  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - The central idea is to refine the optimization strategy by using multiple grid sizes (300 and 600) instead of fixing grid size at 300.\n   - The current best known bound is 0.3808, so we aim to achieve 0.3808 or lower.\n   - We'll run optimization on coarse grid (300) then use its solution to initialize a finer grid (600).\n   - This avoids getting stuck in local optima found by 300-grid optimization and leverages higher resolution for potentially better solutions.\n   - Justification: finer grids can represent more complex h-functions while maintaining the integral constraint and boundaries.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Variables: h[i] \u2208 [0,1] \u2200i, sum(h) = n/2 exactly (where n is grid size)\n   - Objective: min max_k \u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx\n   - Constraints: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, \u222bh(x)dx=1\n   - For grid of size n, we compute correlation using mode='full' (covers all lags)\n   - Optimization uses LBFGS-B, SLSQP, gradient descent and pair search methods\n   - Candidate evaluation: compute C_5 value using actual correlation\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a) If parent available, use initial_h_values (n=300) else create random initial\n   b) First phase - run optimization on 300-grid with full time budget (confirmed to be done)\n   c) Second phase - use best 300-grid solution to initialize 600-grid with adjusted time budget\n   d) Upscale initial condition using linear interpolation of 300-grid solution\n   e) Project interpolated condition to full support and meet integral constraint\n   f) Run optimization methods (L-BFGS-B, SLSQP, gradient descent, pair search) on 600-grid\n   g) Keep best candidate from all phases (including 300-grid and 600-grid outputs)\n   h) Validation: \n        - Check 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i\n        - Verify sum(h)==n_points/2 (adjust by repairing if needed)\n        - Compute C_5 with numerical correlation\n   i) Failure handling: \n        - If first phase times out, fallback to using sample interpolation\n        - For interpolated h, use projection to feasibility if necessary\n        - Time budget allocation: 50% for 300-grid, 50% for 600-grid\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Grid sizes: 300 (base), 600 (upscaled)\n   - Optimization methods: \n        L-BFGS-B with 2000 max iterations\n        SLSQP with 1500 max iterations\n        Gradient descent with adaptive learning rate\n        Pair search with directional moves\n   - Time budget splits:\n        Phase 1 (300-grid): 550 seconds\n        Phase 2 (600-grid): 550 seconds\n   - Interpolation method: linear interpolation of 300-grid solution onto 600-grid\n   - Repair procedure: project_feasible ensures integral and boundaries\n   - Variation: optionally use random interpolation for initial 600-grid if time is short\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Primary task: grid-size independent optimization of h(x)\n   - Key operations: \n        - 300-grid scale: 550s\n        - 600-grid scale: 550s\n        - Operations: correlation calculation, objective functions\n   - Dimensions: \n        - 300-grid: 300 dimensions\n        - 600-grid: 600 dimensions\n   - Budget safety: dividing budget equally gives ~550s per grid (vs previous 1100s for one grid)\n   - Deadline enforcement: \n        - Start each phase at 50% time budget mark\n        - Break and return best candidate if deadline approached\n   - Fallback mechanism:\n        - Use best 300-grid candidate if 600-grid optimization fails\n        - Return '' solution if initial conditions infeasible\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Output format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n   - Checks:\n        1. len(h_values) correct grid size\n        2. 0<=h_values[i]<=1 for all i\n        3. sum(h_values) very close to n/2 (repair if needed)\n        4. c5_bound computed from actual correlation\n   - Return best candidate: global minimum of all performed optimizations\n   - Final score: minimum c5_bound found (lower is better)\n   - Code structure:\n        - Grid-sizes: [300, 600]\n        - Project interpolation: np.interp + project_feasible\n        - Optimization runs: same as original but split by grid size\n        - Objective is same: minimize max correlation\n        - No cross contributions: same as original (7 phases spread over two grids)\n\nRuns will be executed with these parameters. The algorithm will use the parent's solution (if available) to initialize the 600-grid. Validity ensured at return with score computations.",
